// Fluent build blocks — the library half of `@BuildBlock(P)` (fluent spec §6–§9).
//
// The compiler owns the block: which trailing blocks are build blocks, which
// statements are reached expressions, and the lexical `BuildSite` each one
// carries. Everything else — storage, ordering, erasure, identity, diagnostics —
// belongs to the sink written here, in ordinary sx.
//
// A build-accepting function declares its block parameter last:
//
//     column :: (gap: f32, content: @BuildBlock(Drawable)) { ... }
//
// and replays it into a sink:
//
//     content.run(*sink);
//
// Each standalone expression in the block whose type conforms to the protocol
// becomes one `sink.expression(site, initializer)` call. The initializer is an
// `@Init(V)` over the CONCRETE `V`, so the sink reserves `size_of(V)` and writes
// the expression straight into its own storage; nothing is materialized, copied,
// or erased before the sink asks for it.

// The lexical identity of one reached expression (spec §8.1). `id` is the stable
// machine key — derived from the block and the expression's position inside it,
// not from the run, the loop iteration, or the optimization level. `file` /
// `line` / `column` are diagnostic coordinates, and `ordinal` separates
// expressions the coordinates cannot (generated or compact source).
//
// A loop reports the SAME site every iteration: the site is lexical. A library
// that needs per-item identity combines it with an explicit key or an occurrence
// count of its own.
BuildSite :: struct {
    id: u64 = 0;
    file: string = "";
    line: i32 = 0;
    column: i32 = 0;
    ordinal: i32 = 0;
}

// What a sink promises (spec §6.3). An ordinary constraint protocol: no vtable,
// no erasure — `expression` monomorphizes for each concrete `V` the block
// reaches, which is what lets the sink see `V`'s size, alignment, and methods.
//
// The initializer may be written AT MOST ONCE, and a sink that returns without
// writing leaves the source expression unevaluated. A sink that must materialize
// everything simply writes unconditionally.
BuildSink :: protocol(P: Type) constraint {
    expression :: (self: *Self, site: BuildSite, value: @Init($V/P));
}
